\title{Options and environment variables used by condathis}
\description{
\code{condathis} reads a small set of R options and environment variables to
locate \code{micromamba}, choose a backend, and resolve its data directories.
It also builds an isolated environment for every child process it
spawns. That isolation clears or overrides many \verb{CONDA_*} and \verb{MAMBA_*}
variables, so values set in the calling session never reach \code{micromamba}
or the tools it runs.

This page leaves out fallbacks that apply only when the variables listed
here are unset, such as \code{HOMEDRIVE} and \code{HOMEPATH} on Windows.
}
\section{R options}{


Set these with \code{\link[base:options]{base::options()}}, for example in an \code{.Rprofile} file.

\describe{
\item{\code{condathis.micromamba_path}}{
Character string. Path to a \code{micromamba} binary to use instead of
the one managed by \code{condathis}. It has the highest priority in the
\code{micromamba} discovery order (see \emph{micromamba discovery}).
The binary must report a compatible version. Otherwise, \code{condathis}
uses the next candidate. Default: \code{NULL} (unset).
}
\item{\code{condathis.backend_priority}}{
Character vector. The order of the registered backends for
\code{method = "auto"}. A new environment goes to the first available
backend in this order: in \code{\link[=create_env]{create_env()}}, and when \code{\link[=run]{run()}},
\code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}}, \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} or \code{\link[=install_packages]{install_packages()}} create
\code{"condathis-env"}. \code{\link[=list_envs]{list_envs()}} and \code{\link[=get_install_dir]{get_install_dir()}}
report the backends in this order. The vector sets an order, not an
allowlist: registered backends missing from the vector come after the
listed ones, in alphabetical order. \code{condathis} loads a backend
package that it knows, such as \code{"rattler"} of \code{rattlerthis}, when the
vector lists the backend and a new environment needs a backend. The
vector therefore applies even when nothing else loaded the package.
Other names that are not registered are ignored. A value that is not
a character vector without \code{NA} warns once per session with class
\code{condathis_invalid_backend_priority_option} and is ignored.
Default: \code{"micromamba"}, so loading another backend package does not
change where \code{condathis} creates new environments. An existing
environment always uses the backend that owns it.
}
\item{\code{timeout}}{
Numeric. Base R download timeout in seconds. While
\code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}} downloads the binary, \code{condathis} uses the
larger of this value and the \code{timeout} argument as the limit
for each download. The limit applies to both \code{curl} and
\code{\link[utils:download.file]{utils::download.file()}}. \code{Inf}, or \code{0} when both values are \code{0},
means no limit. \code{condathis} lowers a finite limit above 2147483
seconds (about 24.8 days) to that value, because older \code{libcurl}
versions reject larger values. \code{condathis} ignores a session value
that is not a single, non-negative number. It restores the session
value afterwards.
}
}
}

\section{Environment variables read by condathis}{


Set these in the shell, in an \code{.Renviron} file, or with
\code{\link[base:Sys.setenv]{base::Sys.setenv()}}.

\describe{
\item{\code{CONDATHIS_MICROMAMBA_PATH}}{
Path to a \code{micromamba} binary. Same effect as the
\code{condathis.micromamba_path} option, with lower priority than that
option.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_PREFIX}}{
Prefix of the Conda environment active in the calling shell.
\code{condathis} reads it only to look for \code{micromamba} inside that prefix
during discovery. It never passes it on to child processes.
}
\item{\code{PATH}}{
Searched for \code{micromamba} as the last discovery fallback. Also the
base \code{PATH} for child processes. \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}}
prepend the environment's binary directories to it.
}
\item{\code{PATHEXT}}{
Windows only. Extensions tried when a command name is resolved
inside an environment. Default when unset: \verb{.COM;.EXE;.BAT;.CMD}.
}
\item{\code{TAR}}{
A system \code{tar} program, as used by base R: a program name, looked
up on \code{PATH}, or the path of an executable file.
\code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}} extracts the micromamba archive with R's
internal \code{tar}, and uses this program only when that fails. It skips
the value \code{internal}, an empty value, and a program that cannot be
found or run, and uses \code{tar} on \code{PATH} instead. \code{TAR} does not
support arguments.
}
}
}

\section{Environment variables that set data and cache directories}{


\code{condathis} keeps \code{micromamba}, its environments and its package cache
in the condathis data directory, \code{tools::R_user_dir("condathis", "data")} (see \code{\link[=get_install_dir]{get_install_dir()}}). Every other backend keeps its
environments and caches in a directory of its name inside it, such as
\code{R/condathis/rattler} (see \code{\link[=backend_root_dir]{backend_root_dir()}}). These variables move
those directories through the rules of \code{\link[tools:R_user_dir]{tools::R_user_dir()}}. Backends
have no options or environment variables of their own for them.

\describe{
\item{\code{R_USER_DATA_DIR}}{
Base data directory. Takes precedence over \code{XDG_DATA_HOME}. The
condathis data directory is its \code{R/condathis} subdirectory.
}
\item{\code{XDG_DATA_HOME}}{
Base data directory when \code{R_USER_DATA_DIR} is unset. On macOS, when
both are unset, \code{condathis} uses \verb{~/.local/share} instead of the
platform default under \verb{~/Library/Application Support}. The platform
default contains a space, and \verb{micromamba run} can fail on paths
with spaces.
}
\item{\code{R_USER_CACHE_DIR}, \code{XDG_CACHE_HOME}}{
Base cache directory, in that order. \code{\link[=clean_cache]{clean_cache()}} empties its
\code{R/condathis} subdirectory.
}
\item{\code{HOME}, \code{USERPROFILE}, \code{APPDATA}, \code{LOCALAPPDATA}}{
Home and application data directories. \code{\link[tools:R_user_dir]{tools::R_user_dir()}} uses
them when none of the variables above is set: on Windows, \code{APPDATA}
for the data directory and \code{LOCALAPPDATA} for the cache directory.
}
}

\code{condathis} creates the condathis data directory only when a call needs
it. Queries, such as \code{\link[=list_envs]{list_envs()}}, \code{\link[=env_exists]{env_exists()}}, \code{\link[=list_packages]{list_packages()}} and
\code{\link[=validate_env]{validate_env()}}, create no environment and never install \code{micromamba}.
\code{\link[=get_install_dir]{get_install_dir()}} and \code{\link[=get_env_dir]{get_env_dir()}} create only the data directory.

\code{\link[=with_sandbox_dir]{with_sandbox_dir()}} sets all the data and cache variables above to
temporary directories. Environments, packages and the \code{micromamba}
binary that \code{condathis} installs inside it, with every backend, then stay
out of your own directories.
}

\section{Environment variables for downloads}{


\code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}} downloads with \code{\link[curl:curl_download]{curl::curl_download()}} when
\code{curl} 5.0.0 or later is installed, and with \code{\link[utils:download.file]{utils::download.file()}}
otherwise. Both honor the standard proxy variables, such as
\code{http_proxy}, \code{https_proxy}, and \code{no_proxy}.
}

\section{Environment variables set or cleared in child processes}{


Every process \code{condathis} starts, whether \code{micromamba} itself or a
command run by \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} or \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}}, starts from a
copy of the calling session's environment. \code{condathis} then applies the
overrides below to that copy only. \code{condathis} never modifies the calling
R session. A process that you start yourself does not get these
overrides. Pass \code{--no-rc} and \code{--no-env} to \code{micromamba} or \code{conda} to
ignore your own Conda settings.

Cleared or reset, so that the user's own Conda or Mamba setup does not
interfere:

\describe{
\item{\code{CONDARC}, \code{MAMBARC}}{
Set to \code{""}. With the micromamba backend, \code{condathis} also calls
\code{micromamba} with \code{--no-env} and with \code{--rc-file} pointing at
\code{condathis-micromamba.yaml} in the condathis data directory. So
\code{micromamba} ignores the user's configuration files and \verb{MAMBA_*}
settings. That file pins \code{pkgs_dirs} to the \code{condathis} package
cache, and sets \code{shortcuts: false}, so that micromamba adds no Start
Menu shortcuts on Windows. One exception: micromamba extracts
packages in subprocesses (\verb{micromamba package extract}) that it
starts without these options. These subprocesses still read the
user's configuration files, such as \verb{~/.condarc}, \verb{~/.mambarc} and
those in \code{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}. They also read the \verb{MAMBA_*} variables
that are not listed here.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_ROOT_PREFIX}, \code{MAMBA_ROOT_PREFIX}}{
Set to \code{""} for every command of an environment.
\code{MAMBA_ROOT_PREFIX} is set to \code{""} also when the activation of the
micromamba backend sets it, as it does on Windows. \code{micromamba}
itself gets the condathis data directory as its root prefix, with
\code{-r} and in \code{MAMBA_ROOT_PREFIX}. Without \code{MAMBA_ROOT_PREFIX},
micromamba (2.8.1 to 2.10.0 checked) refuses to start, even for
\code{--version}, when its default root prefix exists but is not a Conda
environment. The default root prefix is \verb{$XDG_DATA_HOME/mamba}, else
\verb{~/.local/share/mamba}, on Windows else \code{mamba} in the roaming
application data folder.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_PREFIX}, \code{MAMBA_PREFIX}, \code{CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV},
\code{MAMBA_DEFAULT_ENV}, \code{CONDA_PROMPT_MODIFIER},
\code{MAMBA_PROMPT_MODIFIER}}{
Set to \code{""}.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_SHLVL}, \code{MAMBA_SHLVL}}{
Set to \code{"0"}.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_PKGS_DIRS}, \code{MAMBA_PKGS_DIRS}}{
For \code{micromamba} and the commands of its environments, environments
given by \code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}} included: the \verb{pkgs/} directory of the
condathis data directory. Packages are cached only there. The user's
own \verb{~/.mamba/pkgs} cache is never read or written, and
\code{\link[=clean_cache]{clean_cache()}} never cleans it. For the commands of other backends'
environments: the directory \verb{conda-pkgs/} next to the \verb{envs/}
directory that holds the environment, in the backend's data
directory. A Conda tool run in such an environment caches its
packages there, and \code{\link[=clean_cache]{clean_cache()}} empties it. It is \code{""} when the
environment is not in a directory named \code{envs}. An environment given
by \code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}} that such a backend runs gets the backend's own
\verb{conda-pkgs/}, not one next to the environment. With
\code{activate = TRUE}, the activation of such a backend can set another
value (see \code{\link[=register_backend]{register_backend()}}).
}
\item{\code{MAMBA_ENVS_PATH}, \code{MAMBA_ENVS_DIRS}}{
Set to \code{""}.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_ENVS_DIRS}}{
Removed from the child environment.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_ENVS_PATH}}{
For \code{micromamba} and the commands of its environments, environments
given by \code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}} included: the \verb{envs/} directory of the
condathis data directory. For the commands of other backends'
environments: the parent directory of the environment, and for an
environment given by \code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}}, the backend's own \verb{envs/}
directory. The value is the same with \code{activate = TRUE} and
\code{activate = FALSE}. With activation, the backend can set another
value.
}
\item{\code{R_HOME}}{
Set to \code{""}, so an R installed in a Conda environment does not pick
up the R installation of the calling session.
}
\item{\code{TMPDIR}}{
Set to a new temporary directory for each call. \code{condathis} removes
it when the call returns.
}
\item{\code{TMP}}{
On Windows, for every \code{micromamba} command: a directory in its
\code{TMPDIR}. micromamba writes its temporary files there, such as the
batch files of \verb{micromamba run}, so they are removed with that
directory, also when the command is stopped. The \code{activate.d}
scripts see this value while the activation is recorded. A value
that they build from it names the session's \code{TMP}, unless they
wrote something in that directory: then the value names that
directory, which the command finds in its \code{TMPDIR}, and the
activation is recorded again for every command. The commands of
\code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} keep the session's value,
unless an \code{activate.d} script sets one. \code{TEMP} is never changed.
}
\item{\code{XDG_CACHE_HOME}}{
For every \code{micromamba} command: the \verb{cache/} directory of the
condathis data directory. There micromamba keeps its repodata cache,
instead of in \verb{~/.cache/conda}, and the \code{mamba/proc} directory of
\verb{micromamba run}. The scripts that packages run when they are
installed or removed see this value too. The \code{activate.d} scripts,
while the activation is recorded, and the commands of \code{\link[=run]{run()}},
\code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} keep the session's value.
}
\item{\code{HOME} (\code{USERPROFILE} on Windows)}{
For \verb{micromamba env list}, \verb{micromamba env remove} and, unless it
needs your home directory, \verb{micromamba create}: the \verb{home/}
directory of the condathis data directory. So \code{micromamba} does not
change \verb{~/.conda/environments.txt} (see Details in
\code{\link[=get_install_dir]{get_install_dir()}}). The scripts that packages run when they are
installed or removed by these commands see this value too. On
Windows, micromamba looks there for the Start Menu shortcuts of the
removed packages.
}
\item{\code{PYTHONNOUSERSITE}, \code{PYTHONPATH}, \code{PYTHONHOME}}{
For \verb{micromamba list}, which \code{\link[=list_packages]{list_packages()}} runs:
\code{PYTHONNOUSERSITE} is \code{"1"} and the other two are removed, so pip
reports only the packages of the environment.
}
}

With the default \code{activate = TRUE} of \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and
\code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}}, \code{condathis} applies activation variables on top. For
the micromamba backend, these are the variables that a real
\verb{micromamba run} activation sets. They include the variables from
\code{activate.d} scripts shipped by the installed packages. These scripts run
with the command's \code{TMPDIR}, user cache and working directory.
\code{condathis} records the variables once per environment and R session. It
reuses them until the environment (its packages, its \code{activate.d} scripts
or \code{env_vars.d} files) or the session's environment variables change. It
starts the command itself. Values that name the command's \code{TMPDIR} are
recorded again for every command. Values that name the working directory
are recorded again in another working directory. For other backends, the
backend package sets the activation variables (see \code{\link[=register_backend]{register_backend()}}).

With \code{activate = FALSE}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} applies no activation variables and
only prepends the environment's binary directories to \code{PATH}.
\code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} sets this fixed set instead:

\describe{
\item{\code{CONDA_PREFIX}, \code{CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV}}{
The environment directory and its name. For an environment given by
\code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}}, the name is its directory.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_SHLVL}, \code{MAMBA_SHLVL}}{
Set to \code{"1"}.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_PROMPT_MODIFIER}, \code{MAMBA_PROMPT_MODIFIER}}{
Set to \code{"(<env_name>) "}.
}
\item{\code{PATH}}{
The environment's binary directories, prepended to the session
\code{PATH}.
}
}

\code{CONDA_ENVS_PATH} is the same as with \code{activate = TRUE}: it comes from
the clean variables above.

On Windows, variable names are matched case-insensitively. An override
replaces any inherited spelling of the same name, such as \code{Path}.
}

\section{micromamba discovery}{


\code{condathis} uses the first candidate that exists and reports a
compatible version:
\enumerate{
\item The \code{condathis.micromamba_path} option.
\item The \code{CONDATHIS_MICROMAMBA_PATH} environment variable.
\item The binary managed by \code{condathis}, see \code{\link[=micromamba_bin_path]{micromamba_bin_path()}}.
\item A \code{micromamba} next to the running R, when R is installed in a Conda
environment.
\item A \code{micromamba} inside \code{CONDA_PREFIX}.
\item A \code{micromamba} in the \code{micromamba-env} environment of the micromamba
backend, \verb{<condathis data directory>/envs/micromamba-env}. \code{condathis}
ignores an environment of that name that another backend created.
\item A \code{micromamba} on \code{PATH}.
}

When no candidate is compatible, \code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}} downloads the
pinned version into the managed location.
}

\section{Interactive sessions}{


\code{condathis} shows spinners for every \code{verbose} level except \code{"silent"},
and only when \code{\link[rlang:is_interactive]{rlang::is_interactive()}} returns \code{TRUE}. The same holds
for the download progress of \code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}}. Set the
\code{rlang_interactive} option to \code{FALSE} to turn them off in an interactive
session.
}

